WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.370
SPEAKER: Health care
organizations are increasingly

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:06.000
using cloud platforms to
personalize care, analyze

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.410
large data sets, enhance
research and development,

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.520
optimize operational costs,
and increase their security

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:15.750
and privacy.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.480
And with HIPAA's privacy
rule, health care entities

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.870
are also tasked to safeguard
protected health information.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.330
Google has partnered with
many health care organizations

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.760
over the years and has
consolidated multiple best

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.520
practices into a single solution
called a Healthcare Data

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.510
Engine, a.k.a.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:34.530
HDE, which is aimed at
assisting cloud infrastructure

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.740
or security engineers set up
a data management layer that

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.650
is automated; offer
pre-configured data maps

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.480
and pipelines to
help data engineers

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.840
and clinical informaticists
spend less time on things

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.900
like manual data
transformation processes,

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.600
real-time risk scores,
and insights optimized

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.300
for longitudinal
patient records;

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.580
and has traceability
built in order

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.820
to know where data came from
and how it was processed,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.860
how and why data
exists where it is.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.570
This is known as provenance.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.670
Setting up your data
environment and designing it

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.550
for repeatable deployments
in a highly-regulated sector

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.320
can be challenging.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.260
HDE offers a pre-built
configuration script

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.650
that serves as a template
to help build out your cloud

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:26.100
resources with all the necessary
parameters and governance

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.150
design.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:30.600
It uses a Terraform, which
is a familiar open source way

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.630
to define and provide data
center infrastructure using

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.240
a declarative
configuration language.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.160
When projects are
deployed successfully,

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:39.840
the script will
write a YAML file

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.240
with all generated
fields specified

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.940
in the project's config using
the generated fields path

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:45.960
attribute.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.170
These fields are used to
generate monitoring rules.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:51.540
Overall, Healthcare Data
Engine's implementation

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.140
automates the following for key
dev, staging, and production

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:56.340
environments.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.260
The creation of a
Google Cloud folder

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.870
and multiple cloud
projects, provisions

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.000
the necessary resources
for common health care data

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.960
use cases, as well as the
access rules to manage each.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.449
It establishes a
collection of audit locks,

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:12.390
enables cloud monitoring
metrics and alerts,

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.880
and allows users to
create visualizations

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.150
to track your resources
and security policies.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:21.720
And if an organization uses
an on-premise or third-party

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.750
identity platform, you can
synchronize this user directory

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.970
with Cloud Identity,
as well as set up

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:31.470
SAML 2.0 based single sign on
to let users access Google Cloud

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.960
or any work app by signing
in once and accessing

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.340
all their services.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.520
Next, from an information
harmonization perspective,

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.480
data engineers have a dedicated,
fully-managed Jupyter lab web

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.660
app running on Google Cloud
AI platform notebooks.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.530
That enables them to
convert HL7v2 messages

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:53.820
and proprietary data schemas
that are in CSV into FHIR.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.970
This notebook interface serves
as an integrated development

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.970
tool, because it includes
operations such as syntax

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:03.390
highlighting, auto-completion
of functions, version control,

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:07.140
integration with git, and a
code source repo, et cetera.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.810
And because it is connected to
your Google Cloud resources,

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:12.600
it can execute distributed
data processing pipelines

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:13.530
on Dataflow.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:16.410
Dataflow is a fully-managed
streaming analytics service

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.870
that minimizes latency,
processing time,

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:23.680
and cost through auto
scaling and batch processing.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.670
This is the Jupyter
Lab IDE UI in HDE.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.860
We will open Big Query
here on the side,

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.570
thanks to the UI plugin.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.940
We now have a list of
tables listed inside

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.490
of a Big Query data set
provisioned through the HDE

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:36.540
process.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.000
These tables have been
pre-ingested with raw CSV data.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.280
Let's take a look at them.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:44.550
Note that our goal is to
convert this CSV patient

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.170
data into a FHIR JSON resource.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:49.510
Next, let's look at
our local file system.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.960
These are prepackaged
sample mapping files.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.600
They are part of
HDE's Jupyter Lab IDE.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.810
This one specifically
converts CSV data to FHIR.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.040
Now let's visit Git.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.400
To the Jupyter Demo branch,
and open the following file.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.950
Now from this notebook, we
will execute these commands

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:08.190
in Python code, then we will
run this prebuilt magic command.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:14.520
When it's done, we visit the
JSON that has been generated.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.400
Next, we run a validation
test to our FHIR resource

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.070
and find an error saying that
the patient given name is

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.150
expected to be an array.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:37.220
So we will modify the code
responsible for patient data

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:40.370
by converting it to an array
and rerun the magic command

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:41.225
and reload the JSON.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.140
And now we have it
validated as successful,

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:58.600
because it is now valid FHIR.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:00.580
After this step, we
perform test mapping,

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:02.410
which executes the
data transformation

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.630
code into a Dataflow pipeline.

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:08.950
This link brings you to
the Dataflow pipeline.

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.560
And finally, we return to
Git and look at the changes,

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:12.610
and then commit them.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.980
Data engineers also
need traceability

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.300
of how data is
transformed and created.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.480
They need to debug data
issues and understand which

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:25.890
pipeline produced what data.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:28.230
This is commonly referred
to as provenance.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:30.840
Provenance data gets written
to Google Cloud Storage

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.660
by the various pipelines for
ingestion, harmonization,

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:35.160
or reconciliation.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.400
A cron job using Cloud Scheduler
runs a processing pipeline

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.590
that takes this provenance
data and writes it

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.990
to an operational FHIR store.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:46.920
Provenance links device to
input and outputs, document

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.410
references, and FHIR resources.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:50.970
For example, let's
figure out how

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:54.390
a sample patient got created
into the FHIR file store.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:57.540
By looking at the JSON,
we see several attributes.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.880
An important one
is the ID field,

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.925
which can help us understand the
provenance of the patient data.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:09.130
Let's visit the
operational FHIR store

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:10.840
and look at the
provenance record

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:15.700
by using the filterable
lookup of the patient ID.

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:19.150
As we locate it, we can
investigate in the Elements tab

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:23.260
the additional fields
tied to that record.

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:26.260
The provenance record combines
the source information, as well

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.330
as the data of the
pipeline that transformed

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:30.910
the source into the target,
as well as the target that

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:32.090
created the source.

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:34.480
For example, I can see
there are 19 resources that

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.360
were created in conjunction
with the patient.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:41.410
Some are organizational, device,
location, or message resources.

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:48.690
As for the pipeline
itself, it exists

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:52.170
as a device resource under
the Agent field, then "who."

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:54.910
In order for me to figure
out the message itself,

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:58.140
which HL7v2 message was
the source for the data,

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:00.750
I can go to the
entity, what field,

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:03.030
and here I have a
document reference that

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:06.060
points to the HL7v2 message.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.060
Let me click into it and
show you how it's structured.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.520
When I click Content,
Attachment, URL,

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.480
I have a pointer to the
message in the HL7v2 store.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.550
And if I were to do
a cURL Get request,

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:19.410
I would retrieve
the full message.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:23.460
And that is an overview of
how provenance works in HDE.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.050
And there you have
it, a quick summary

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:27.300
on how you can enable
infrastructure and data

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.600
specialists via the
Healthcare Data Engine, which

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.670
is a predefined
configuration to get you

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.010
started with the necessary
Cloud infrastructure

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:38.730
and data transformations
with built-in auditability.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:41.160
To get started with some of
the underlying technology

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.950
that powers HDE, you will need
to have a Google Cloud project.

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:47.670
If you do not have one, I have
included a link to a trial

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:49.980
account with free credits
in this video's description,

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:52.380
along with other
helpful resources.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:54.760
And community, if you
found this episode helpful,

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:57.150
please subscribe to the
channel to get notifications

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:58.560
of more health care episodes.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.110
Cheers.

